WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:18.140
The 
year is 30 BC.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.119
In the grand Egyptian capital of Alexandria,
panic is sweeping the streets.

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:28.960
A mighty Roman fleet is bearing down on the
city, bringing fire, destruction.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.970
In a half-finished mausoleum, Cleopatra and
her lover Mark Antony await the inevitable.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.350
Antony’s stomach has already been sliced
open, and now the great general is merely

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.950
waiting to die.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.680
As Roman boots pound the streets, the wounded
man orders one last cup of wine.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.820
Then, as his lover looks on, he offers her
a final toast before everything goes black.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.500
The deaths of Mark Antony and Cleopatra are
some of the most famous in all history.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.939
Breathlessly poured over by the ancients,
turned into high tragedy by Shakespeare, the

00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:05.909
extinguishing of their love has been fodder
for countless adaptations.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:09.030
But how much do most of us know about Mark
Antony?

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.340
How did he come to be Cleopatra’s lover,
and how did the two wind up dying in misery,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.840
their dreams just so much ash?

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.780
Today, Biographics is uncovering the story
of Mark Antony; the man whose death heralded

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.719
the birth of an empire.

00:01:28.719 --> 00:01:32.509
The Boy and the King
One of the most interesting things about Mark

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:38.100
Antony’s early career is that it basically
reads like a “greatest hits of ancient history.”

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.130
Seriously, over the next six minutes, you’re
gonna watch him meet Cleopatra, Julius Caesar,

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:47.829
and the future emperor Augustus; and see him
cross Rubicons and help conquer Gaul.

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:52.670
But perhaps the most remarkable thing about
a guy who spent, like, his entire life at

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:58.399
the epicenter of history is just how unremarkable
he seemed destined to be.

00:01:58.399 --> 00:02:05.179
Born on January 14, 83 BC, Antony came from
a family that was rich, but not respected.

00:02:05.179 --> 00:02:08.149
His father was a famously incompetent general.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:13.220
While his mother was a cousin of Julius Caesar,
Caesar in 83 BC was just some 17-year old

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.520
who nobody expected to one day become dictator.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.890
It also didn’t help that Antony’s early
life was remarkably dissolute.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:25.870
As a teenager, he fell in with a guy called
Curio, who was all about blowing Antony’s

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.330
money on whores, games, and gold.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:35.370
As a result, Antony wound up 250 talents in
debt before he was 20, a sum that’s equal

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:37.780
to something like $5 million today.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.070
So Antony skipped town, heading to Greece
to “study philosophy”.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:46.990
You know those guys who just keep partying
after college until it becomes kinda embarrassing?

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.010
That was Mark Antony in Greece.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:53.220
But his bohemian ways did give him something
more than an endless hangover.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.760
They left him with an abiding love of Hellenistic
culture.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:02.360
Finally, when Antony was 26, a Roman general
called Aulus Gabinius convinced him to get

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.260
his life in shape by joining the war in Syria.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.100
Antony agreed, perhaps hoping to find more
places he could get drunk in.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.650
What he found instead was his vocation.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.290
It turned out Antony was great at war.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.890
As a cavalryman in Syria, he distinguished
himself again and again.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:24.100
Likewise in Egypt, where the Romans had been
invited to crush revolts against Ptolemy XII.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:28.520
It was while doing all that revolt-crushing
that Antony was introduced to one of Ptolemy’s

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.720
teenage daughters.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.030
The girl was only 14.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.080
But something about her charmed the young
soldier, hypnotized him.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.980
The name of that teenage girl?

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.450
You guessed it: Cleopatra.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.040
But this is just a quick cameo.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.180
Antony didn’t immediately shack up with
Cleo, but instead went off to fight in Gaul

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.930
- a place roughly analogous to modern France
and Belgium.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:54.850
There, he served under the second of the great
figures who’d shape his life: Julius Caesar.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:04:00.570
By 54 BC, Caesar was no longer a 17-year old
nobody, but a great general and, along with

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:05.150
Pompey and another dude you don’t need to
bother remembering, a member of the First

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.110
Triumvirate that called the shots in Rome.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.240
In short, he was well on his path to greatness.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.180
Which made Antony’s arrival perfect timing.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.880
Under Caesar, Antony continued to excel at
warring.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:21.169
Not only that, he also excelled at just being
a soldier; drinking with his men, eating alongside

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:24.550
them, and earning himself a devoted following
among the rank and file.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.270
By 50 BC and the end of the conquest of Gaul,
Antony had proven himself so many times over

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.800
that Caesar sent the 33-year old back to Rome
to take the office of tribune.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.690
But while being tribune would’ve been a
fitting cap for many Romans’ careers, Mark

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:39.930
Antony was just getting started.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.569
In barely a year he was going to be ruling
the city itself.

00:04:44.569 --> 00:04:52.830
The Man and the Dictator
When Antony arrived, Rome was in turmoil.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:56.650
Caesar’s fellow Triumvir Pompey was trying
to force the great general to give up his

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.270
army, and there were frequent clashes in the
streets between the their supporters.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:05.050
Onto this political dumpster fire, Antony
poured a cascade of gasoline.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:09.210
In the Senate, Antony hooked back up with
his old buddy Curio - now a Caesar supporter

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.669
- and began goading Pompey.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:15.669
Things came to a head when Antony suggested
Pompey get out of politics for the good of

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:20.460
the Republic, leading to so many death threats
that Antony was forced to flee - all the way

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.580
back up to the frontiers of Italy, where Caesar
was camped across a river called the Rubicon.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.020
You know the phrase “crossing the Rubicon”?

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.169
It means to commit to a course of action from
which there’s no going back.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:36.520
We have this phrase because, in 49 BC, it
was forbidden for a commander like Caesar

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:39.969
to march his troops over the river and into
Italy.

00:05:39.969 --> 00:05:43.210
But when Caesar heard Antony’s story, he
decided he had no choice.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:49.029
So he elevated Antony to his second in command,
and then both literally and figuratively crossed

00:05:49.029 --> 00:05:50.169
the Rubicon.

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:51.510
Mark Antony, everyone.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.370
Witness to both major historic events and
the coining of everyday phrases.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.680
The news of Caesar’s crossing caused Pompey
to flee Rome.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.330
This was great from the perspective of Caesar
being able to waltz in and have himself declared

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:08.759
dictator, but less good from the perspective
of Caesar now having to track down his rival.

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:12.699
But before Caesar gave chase, he put the city
under Antony’s control.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:17.590
As supreme administrator of Rome itself, Antony
was almost comically useless.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:22.460
Plutarch writes that “he was too lazy to
pay attention” to running the city, and

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:24.470
that’s being generous.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:27.840
Antony was so negligent he almost caused a
revolution.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.830
When Caesar returned from fighting Pompey
in Egypt in 46 BC, he was so stunned by what

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:36.930
a crappy job his number two was doing that
he kicked him out the city.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.039
For Antony, this was a real double whammy.

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:44.809
See, Caesar hadn’t returned from Egypt alone,
but with Antony’s old flame Cleopatra in

00:06:44.809 --> 00:06:45.889
tow.

00:06:45.889 --> 00:06:50.599
Only Cleopatra was now a client of Caesar
- which meant he’d put her on the throne,

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:54.650
and she in turn had been impregnated with
his son, Caesarion.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:58.550
When Antony was exiled to Gaul, he wasn’t
just stewing over his broken relationship

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:02.680
with Caesar, but also Caesar shacking up with
his crush.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:06.300
Sadly for Antony, this double whammy was about
to become a triple hit.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:12.590
In 45 BC, Caesar was forced to go fight the
Sons of Pompey in Hispania - i.e. modern Spain.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:17.029
But with Antony in disgrace, he needed a replacement
by his side.

00:07:17.029 --> 00:07:19.479
The man he chose?

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:24.029
His 18-year old great-nephew Octavian; the
future emperor Augustus.

00:07:24.029 --> 00:07:28.889
It was the beginning of a rivalry between
Antony and Octavian that would shake the known

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:29.919
world.

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:38.279
Death of an (almost) King
So, now might be a good time to talk about

00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:39.279
Octavian.

00:07:39.279 --> 00:07:43.479
Born in 63 BC, Octavian was twenty years younger
than Mark Antony.

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:47.989
Like Antony, he had a family connection with
Caesar, who was his grandma’s brother.

00:07:47.989 --> 00:07:52.369
But Octavian never saw his famous great-uncle,
and may not have made much an impression if

00:07:52.369 --> 00:07:53.369
he had.

00:07:53.369 --> 00:07:55.600
Octavian was weak and sickly.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.639
In fact, when 45 BC rolled around and Caesar
asked Octavian to accompany him to Hispania,

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:02.090
Octavian was too ill to go.

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:07.469
But what Octavian lacked in muscles of steel,
he made up for in his iron will.

00:08:07.469 --> 00:08:12.150
The boy was ambitious, and he knew the path
to greatness lay through Caesar.

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.289
As soon as he was capable of moving, he dragged
himself to Hispania to witness the end of

00:08:16.289 --> 00:08:17.289
the war.

00:08:17.289 --> 00:08:21.759
Pleased his weak nephew had put himself in
such danger, Caesar honored Octavian by letting

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:23.499
him ride home alongside him.

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:27.180
It was likely during this journey that the
fate of Rome was transformed.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:28.960
But we’ll get to that soon.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.760
While all this was happening, Antony was still
sitting in miserable exile in Gaul.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:39.500
And there he might have stayed, had Caesar
not swung by to see him on his way back to

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.500
Rome.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.630
It was now almost two years since Caesar had
knocked up Antony’s lady and kicked him

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:47.759
out, so we like to imagine the greetings were
super awkward.

00:08:47.759 --> 00:08:50.200
But the two men soon overcame their differences.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.759
Antony was a dyed-in-the-wool Caesar loyalist.

00:08:52.759 --> 00:08:56.610
And old Julius never could say no to a sycophant.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.440
He asked Antony to return with him to the
Eternal City.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:05.639
And so it was that, in 45 BC, three of the
most important men in history traveled together

00:09:05.639 --> 00:09:06.720
across Gaul.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.550
But all was not well back in Rome.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.750
After the Rubicon thing, Caesar’s enemies
were pretty sure he meant to make himself

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:14.750
into a king.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.630
Not that Antony helped matters.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:21.720
In February, 44 BC, Caesar was presiding over
a religious ceremony when Antony suddenly

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:27.010
clambered up towards him, pulled out a laurel
crown, and tried to declare his boss King

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:28.010
of Rome.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.430
Caesar waved him off, but the damage had already
been done.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:35.440
Here was the dictator’s number two, literally
trying to crown him king.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:40.000
If you were a Republican-minded senator back
then, what would you have done?

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:45.170
On March 15, 44 BC - the Ides of March - the
Senate summoned Caesar.

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:49.110
As Caesar was on his way, Antony received
a warning that something was about to happen,

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.720
and rushed to tell his boss.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:54.779
He actually caught sight of him, walking into
the Senate, and tried to shout a warning.

00:09:54.779 --> 00:10:00.060
But the crowds were too heavy, and his voice
was lost on the bustle of Rome’s streets.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:03.040
Antony would never see his mentor alive again.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.110
Julius Caesar was stabbed to death that day,
killed by his own Senate.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:11.560
In the aftermath of the assassination, Antony
fled Rome, convinced he was next.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:13.160
But Antony was wrong.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:16.260
The plotters had never thought to kill off
Caesar’s loyalists.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:19.300
Antony, Octavian… all were left alive.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.750
When Antony finally clicked that no-one was
coming for him, he returned to Rome and reassumed

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.230
his position of Consul.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:29.209
The first thing he did was open Caesar’s
will, curious to see what this man he’d

00:10:29.209 --> 00:10:31.110
served so loyally had left him.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.480
What Caesar had left him was a punch in the
gut.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.560
Caesar’s will left nothing to Antony.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:42.250
But it did leave a huge fortune to Octavian,
as well as posthumously adopting the boy,

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.050
turning him into Caesar’s only Roman son.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:49.160
Suddenly, Antony was no longer the biggest
man left standing, no longer the obvious heir

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:50.160
to the dictator.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:52.880
There was now a powerful rival for Antony
in Rome.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:12:02.470
And there was only one way this rivalry could
ever end.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:19.640
The Triumvirate Reborn
If the conspirators ever had considered killing

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:23.200
Antony, they likely spent Caesar’s funeral
wishing they’d done it.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:27.200
During the procession, Antony held Caesar’s
bloodstained toga high, and gave a thunderous

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:29.260
speech denouncing those who’d murdered him.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.510
The crowd was whipped into such a frenzy that
the conspirators were forced to flee.

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.740
But Antony didn’t really want vengeance.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.680
What he wanted was to take over Caesar’s
old job.

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:44.149
So after getting the crowds riled up, he began
meeting with the Senate, trying to negotiate

00:12:44.149 --> 00:12:45.149
a peaceful solution.

00:12:45.149 --> 00:12:46.930
Unfortunately, he hadn’t counted on Octavian.

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:51.980
When Octavian first heard of Caesar’s assassination
from Antony, he’d also heard that the dictator

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:54.470
had willed all his possessions to the Roman
people.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:59.600
When he found out Caesar actually left everything
to him, he’d figured out what Antony’s

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:00.600
game was.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.529
From that point on, Octavian did everything
in his power to hobble the older man.

00:13:04.529 --> 00:13:09.300
His opening salvo was to reveal to the riled-up
mobs that Antony was doing a deal with the

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:10.300
Senate.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:14.800
Octavian whipped them up into such a fury
that Antony was forced to beat a retreat from

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.750
peace and declare himself on the path of vengeance.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:24.110
The upshot was that, in January, 43 BC, Antony
rode out at the head of an army to Cisalpine

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:28.360
Gaul, where the assassin Decimus Brutus was
lying low.

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:33.120
And no, that’s not the famous Brutus, just
another assassin of Caesar also called Brutus.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:34.120
Go figure.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:39.699
Anyway, long story short: the Senate got annoyed
at Antony’s unilateral action, and sent

00:13:39.699 --> 00:13:41.389
Octavian to stop him.

00:13:41.389 --> 00:13:47.110
Instead, the two rivals joined forces, destroyed
Brutus’s army, and drove him into the wilds.

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:50.519
A few days later, a local tribe sent a box
to Antony.

00:13:50.519 --> 00:13:52.370
Inside it lay Brutus’s head.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:57.879
Shortly thereafter, Antony and Octavian met
on a small island under the eye of the mutually-trusted

00:13:57.879 --> 00:13:58.879
Lepidus.

00:13:58.879 --> 00:14:02.970
The fight with B-list Brutus had shown both
men how powerful they could be together.

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:06.050
Although they despised one another, neither
was stupid.

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:09.080
They knew that, alone, they might annihilate
themselves…

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.839
But, together, they could take the whole of
Rome.

00:14:11.839 --> 00:14:14.600
And so the Second Triumvirate was born.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:19.550
Forged between Antony, Octavian, and their
friend Lepidus, the Second Triumvirate would

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.670
rule Rome for ten years.

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:23.870
They were effectively invincible.

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:27.430
When they returned to Rome, the Senate basically
just handed them the keys to the city.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:31.990
When the Triumvirs announced the property
of 2,000 Republicans was now forfeit to fund

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:37.319
the coming war with the real Brutus and his
co-assassin Cassius, everyone just looked

00:14:37.319 --> 00:14:38.439
the other way.

00:14:38.439 --> 00:14:39.480
But make no mistake.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:45.360
The Second Triumvirate was not an end in and
of itself, but a stepping stone.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.230
All three parties wanted to rule Rome solo.

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.870
All three knew that, at some point, they’d
have to kill their rivals.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:53.889
The only question was when.

00:14:53.889 --> 00:15:00.020
In 42 BC, Antony and Octavian finally rode
east to confront the assassins, leaving Lepidus

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:01.020
in Rome.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:04.520
The first battle came in September, and only
resulted in a draw.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:09.930
A brilliant maneuver by Antony captured Cassius’s
camp, but an equally brilliant act of stupidity

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:14.720
by Octavian led to Antony’s camp simultaneously
falling to Cassius.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:19.510
But none of that actually mattered, because
Cassius was watching all this from a hill

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.779
with poor visibility and failed to see his
own victory.

00:15:22.779 --> 00:15:26.290
So, thinking that it was all over, he ordered
a slave to kill him.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:27.850
Which left only Marcus Brutus.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:29.509
He of “et tu, Brute?”

00:15:29.509 --> 00:15:30.509
fame.

00:15:30.509 --> 00:15:33.360
On October 23, the Triumvirs forced a final
battle.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:34.710
This time, there would be no draw.

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:36.319
Brutus’s army was crushed.

00:15:36.319 --> 00:15:40.850
In the aftermath, the assassin climbed a nearby
hill, pulled his sword, and drove it into

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.019
his own abdomen.

00:15:42.019 --> 00:15:43.490
Brutus was dead.

00:15:43.490 --> 00:15:44.490
The war was over.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:48.560
To celebrate, Antony and Octavian divided
the empire between themselves: the west - minus

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.709
Gaul - going to Octavian, while the east went
to Antony.

00:15:51.709 --> 00:15:55.660
As Octavian headed back for Rome, Antony was
left wondering what to do.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:59.950
He was now one of the two most influential
men on Earth, lord of all that lay east of

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:01.029
the Adriatic.

00:16:01.029 --> 00:16:04.519
What’s a guy to do given all that power?

00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:06.740
Why, what any guy would do of course!

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:11.510
Antony went off to impress a girl.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:16.529
The Triumvir and the Queen
And so here we are.

00:16:16.529 --> 00:16:21.779
After over half the video, we finally get
to the part of Antony’s life everyone remembers.

00:16:21.779 --> 00:16:23.769
His relationship with Cleopatra.

00:16:23.769 --> 00:16:27.480
That the two became lovers at all is purely
down to luck.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:31.999
While Antony might have been smitten by the
Egyptian queen years ago, Cleopatra tended

00:16:31.999 --> 00:16:36.079
not to have relationships out of love, but
out of necessity.

00:16:36.079 --> 00:16:40.939
Come 41 BC, though, what Cleopatra needed
was a guarantee from Rome that it would continue

00:16:40.939 --> 00:16:42.570
to prop up her throne.

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:46.639
And what better way to secure that guarantee
than by seducing a Triumvir?

00:16:46.639 --> 00:16:51.379
Although Antony was the one who arranged their
meeting, it was Cleopatra who controlled it.

00:16:51.379 --> 00:16:56.170
She arrived at Tarsus on a boat with silver
oars, dressed as Aphrodite, while girls waited

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:59.279
on her every whim and Greek music played.

00:16:59.279 --> 00:17:03.269
For Anthony, who’d become obsessed with
both Cleopatra and Hellenistic culture in

00:17:03.269 --> 00:17:07.829
his youth, this must’ve been like watching
all his late night fantasies come true.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:12.760
In fact, Antony was so smitten that he accompanied
the queen back to Alexandria that winter,

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:14.770
where the two formalized their alliance.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:20.260
But while “Clantony” or “Antopatra”
may have started as an alliance of convenience,

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.680
it soon grew into something bigger.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:26.480
That winter in Alexandria, the Queen and the
Triumvir were inseparable.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:30.590
They started a group called “The society
of inimitable livers,” where they drank

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:34.630
as much fine wine and eat as much fancy food
as they could stomach.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:38.800
They went out in the streets at night together,
wearing disguises so no-one would recognize

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:39.800
them.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:41.000
By day they hunted.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.929
And slowly, inevitably, they began to fall
in love.

00:17:43.929 --> 00:17:46.720
Not that Antony had taken his eyes off the
prize.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:50.970
That same winter, Antony’s family in Rome
tried to foment rebellion against Octavian

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.120
- likely on Antony’s orders.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:58.360
But Octavian survived the rebellion and not
only exacted ruthless revenge, but took Antony’s

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.630
province of Gaul for himself.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:05.090
This very nearly led to yet another civil
war, but neither Antony nor Octavian was strong

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:06.110
enough yet.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:07.110
But make no mistake.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:10.270
The blood between these two was worse than
it had ever been.

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:15.110
When the Second Triumvirate’s term was renewed
in 37 BC, part of the deal was that Antony

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:19.299
would trade 120 ships for four of Octavian’s
legions.

00:18:19.299 --> 00:18:23.190
Antony sent the ships off to Rome… only
to realize too late that there would be no

00:18:23.190 --> 00:18:24.529
legions coming.

00:18:24.529 --> 00:18:25.710
Octavian had tricked him.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:30.380
Yet Antony still couldn’t just march on
Rome and destroy his young rival, just as

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:33.309
Octavian couldn’t barge into Alexandria.

00:18:33.309 --> 00:18:35.760
What Antony needed was a PR triumph.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.900
Something that would turn the whole of the
Rome into his fanboys.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:43.070
Something like a glorious victory in war.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:47.059
In 36 BC, Antony invaded Parthia.

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:49.140
The choice was wonderfully symbolic.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:52.390
In 53 BC, Parthia had humiliated Rome.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:57.090
Julius Caesar had actually been preparing
to attack right before his assassination.

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:02.620
If Antony could finish what Caesar started,
there’d be no doubt about who was Julius’s

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:04.260
real heir.

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.120
Sadly, that was a big “if”.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:10.910
For the Romans, invading Parthia was kind
of like modern European dictators invading

00:19:10.910 --> 00:19:13.470
Russia - it rarely worked out.

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:19.440
Antony marched in, expecting easy victory…
...and instead watched as brutal weather killed

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:21.480
over a quarter of his soldiers.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.360
He was forced to retreat without fighting
a single battle.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:28.539
The bodged invasion was so humiliating that
Antony seriously considered suicide.

00:19:28.539 --> 00:19:33.210
In the end, though, he simply slunk back to
Alexandria, to take comfort in Cleopatra’s

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:34.210
arms.

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.380
There would always be another chance.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:39.360
He just had to lick his wounds and try again
later.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:41.210
But Antony was wrong.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:42.610
There wouldn’t be another chance.

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:47.330
His fate had already been sealed.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:53.840
Octavian Rises
If the Invasion of Parthia was a PR blunder,

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:58.950
then what Antony did next was like live-streaming
a video of himself wearing swastikas while

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.550
kicking puppies.

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:04.870
Back in Alexandria, Antony began to dress
more and more in eastern fashion.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:10.340
He began acting not like a staid, boring Roman,
but like a hedonistic Greek.

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:13.220
And this infuriated the staid and boring Romans!

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.929
Going native just wasn’t done.

00:20:14.929 --> 00:20:20.240
On top of that, Antony started calling Cleopatra
“queen of queens,” a warning sign that

00:20:20.240 --> 00:20:25.630
the Romans might have to put up with a non-Roman
empress if Antony defeated Octavian.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.399
But Antony’s biggest mistake was probably
what he did with Caesarion.

00:20:29.399 --> 00:20:30.620
Remember Caesarion?

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:34.980
He was the lovechild of Julius Caesar and
Cleopatra who got, like, a one second introduction

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.830
toward the start of this incredibly-crammed
video.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:42.850
Well, Antony declared that not only was Caesarion
“king of kings,” but that he was also

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:44.170
the true heir to Caesar.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:48.480
For Octavian, this was like a gigantic warning
sign flashing “YOU WILL NEED TO KILL THIS

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.190
DUDE ASAP.”

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:52.480
So Octavian prepared his final push.

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:57.580
First, he removed Lepidus from the Triumvirate,
consolidating his power in the western empire.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:02.480
Second, he started a fierce propaganda war,
painting Antony as a weak general under the

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:04.630
spell of a wicked queen.

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:07.120
From Alexandria, Antony tried to fire back.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:12.409
He started a rumor that Caesar only adopted
Octavian because the boy had seduced the dictator.

00:21:12.409 --> 00:21:15.690
But Octavian was just better at this kind
of scheming.

00:21:15.690 --> 00:21:20.230
When the Second Triumvirate’s term expired,
he announced he no longer wanted the title.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:24.659
When Antony hesitated about dissolving the
Triumvirate, Octavian was able to paint him

00:21:24.659 --> 00:21:26.279
as a power hungry tyrant.

00:21:26.279 --> 00:21:27.970
Oh, the irony.

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:30.700
Things finally crumbled in 32 BC.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:35.750
Octavian stormed into the sacred temple holding
Antony’s will and removed it, breaking every

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:37.570
taboo in Roman society.

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:41.550
But when he read it to the masses, and it
turned out Antony had done even worse.

00:21:41.550 --> 00:21:48.049
In his will, Antony had left the entire eastern
part of the empire to Cleopatra and her sons.

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:50.610
This was no longer just going native.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:51.779
This was treason.

00:21:51.779 --> 00:21:55.529
In a bruising session, the Senate declared
war on Antony, granting Octavian the power

00:21:55.529 --> 00:21:57.030
to fight his rival.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:00.409
Down in Alexandria, Antony realized the time
had come.

00:22:00.409 --> 00:22:05.950
Taking 400 ships, he appointed Cleopatra his
co-general, then sailed out in an awe-inspiring

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:07.090
show of force.

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:09.429
Sadly, a show was all it was.

00:22:09.429 --> 00:22:12.170
For whatever reason, Antony was wary of attacking
Rome directly.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:16.909
So he hung back, wintering his ships to the
south, in a little bay with only a narrow

00:22:16.909 --> 00:22:17.909
entrance.

00:22:17.909 --> 00:22:20.500
In effect, this bay became his own noose.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:24.670
In spring, 31 BC, Octavian’s ships managed
to blockade Antony inside the bay.

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:27.900
At the same time, a huge overland army cut
off their escape.

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:32.260
For the next half year, Antony and his men
were trapped in a state of siege, with too

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:35.840
little water, too little food, and way too
much disease.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:40.340
Finally, realizing his entire army was on
the verge of deserting, Antony forced a battle

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:41.590
on September 2.

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:45.340
The Battle of Actium is today a byword for
humiliating defeat.

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:50.110
Octavian’s ships pummeled Antony’s weakened
navy, reducing it to so much driftwood.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:54.020
It was only a chance break in Octavian’s
line that allowed Antony and Cleopatra to

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:57.590
personally escape in a fast boat, high-tailing
it back to Egypt.

00:22:57.590 --> 00:22:59.750
But there would be no escape now.

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.110
No second chances.

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.850
Octavian was coming for them.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:06.990
And he wouldn’t rest until Antony was dead.

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:14.100
“We are for the Dark”
The end of Mark Antony’s life is one of

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:18.240
the saddest examples of refusing to accept
reality that you’ll ever see.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:23.470
At first, Antony and Cleopatra seem to have
decided they could reason with Octavian, and

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:25.529
spent all winter sending him envoys.

00:23:25.529 --> 00:23:30.159
When Octavian laughed them off, they began
preparing a clearly doomed defense of Alexandria.

00:23:30.159 --> 00:23:33.909
Yet neither seems to have realized the writing
was on the wall.

00:23:33.909 --> 00:23:38.320
As they waited for winter to pass, Antony
and Cleopatra repeatedly got drunk and talked

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:41.059
about what they would do once they’d finally
defeated Octavian.

00:23:41.059 --> 00:23:46.980
It was like hearing two pigs discuss what
they’d do once they’d taken over the slaughterhouse:

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.940
depressing and amusing in equal measure.

00:23:49.940 --> 00:23:55.190
At long last, in the final days of July, 30
BC, Octavian’s fleet was spotted on the

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:56.190
horizon.

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:59.970
On August 1, Antony sent out the remnants
of his navy to meet them while he amassed

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:01.289
an army on land.

00:24:01.289 --> 00:24:06.159
His plan was to give Octavian’s ships hell,
and then die gloriously in battle, a soldier

00:24:06.159 --> 00:24:07.159
to the end.

00:24:07.159 --> 00:24:09.299
Alas, fate had other plans.

00:24:09.299 --> 00:24:14.149
As soon as they approached Octavian’s ships,
Antony’s navy raised the white flag of surrender.

00:24:14.149 --> 00:24:17.741
On land, his army saw what was happening,
thought the Ancient equivalent of “to Hell

00:24:17.741 --> 00:24:21.559
with this,” dropped their weapons and mass
deserted.

00:24:21.559 --> 00:24:27.409
The only one left was Mark Antony, standing
impotently on the hot Alexandrian shore, watching

00:24:27.409 --> 00:24:28.409
death approach.

00:24:28.409 --> 00:24:33.080
Humiliated, the great general tried to return
to Cleopatra’s palace and see her one last

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:34.080
time.

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:37.840
But Cleopatra had sensed which way the winds
were blowing and had gone into hiding in an

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:39.399
unfinished mausoleum.

00:24:39.399 --> 00:24:43.019
She made one of her aides tell Antony that
she’d already committed suicide.

00:24:43.019 --> 00:24:47.289
Faced with the loss of his empire, of his
queen, and of certain death, Antony did the

00:24:47.289 --> 00:24:48.640
only thing he could.

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:51.409
He drew his dagger and stabbed himself in
the stomach.

00:24:51.409 --> 00:24:55.409
Then he slid down onto the floor, bleeding
and in agony.

00:24:55.409 --> 00:24:57.870
But the story isn’t quite over yet.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:00.149
Stomach wounds can take a long time to kill
you.

00:25:00.149 --> 00:25:04.340
When Cleopatra heard what Antony had done,
she changed her mind and sent for him.

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:08.500
The dying man was dragged to her mausoleum,
leaving a trail of blood in his wake.

00:25:08.500 --> 00:25:11.789
There, he was propped up, facing his queen
for the final time.

00:25:11.789 --> 00:25:14.809
At the sight of her lover, Cleopatra burst
into tears.

00:25:14.809 --> 00:25:18.240
Antony briefly tried to comfort her, but thought
better of it.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:21.110
With his last ounce of strength, he demanded
wine be brought.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:25.300
He took the cup, toasted his queen, and then
drank for the final time.

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:30.080
We don’t know Mark Antony’s real last
words, but Shakespeare had him declare:

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:34.899
“The miserable change now at my end
Lament nor sorrow at; but please your thoughts

00:25:34.899 --> 00:25:40.820
In feeding them with those my former fortunes
Wherein I lived, the greatest prince o' the

00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:45.039
world,
The noblest; and do now not basely die,

00:25:45.039 --> 00:25:49.860
Not cowardly put off my helmet to
My countryman,--a Roman by a Roman

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.440
Valiantly vanquish'd.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:55.000
Now my spirit is going;
I can no more.”

00:25:55.000 --> 00:26:00.510
Mark Antony died on August 1, 30 BC, just
hours before Octavian captured Alexandria.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:05.240
In the aftermath, the 33-year old had Caesarion
killed, before annexing Egypt into his empire.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:09.659
Although he allowed Cleopatra to live, he
let her know it would only be as a miserable

00:26:09.659 --> 00:26:14.980
wretch, without money or power, rotting in
some obscure Roman province.

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:17.820
Cleopatra committed suicide on August 30.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:19.890
She was the last pharaoh of Egypt.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:24.840
Just three years later, in 27 BC, Octavian
assumed the name Augustus, becoming the first

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:25.920
Roman emperor.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:31.470
Already, most had forgotten Mark Antony, forgotten
the man who came so close to ruling the empire.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:32.470
But not everyone.

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:37.070
In the 2,000-plus years since his death, Mark
Antony has become a household name, one of

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.220
the few ancients almost everyone remembers.

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:42.850
Partly, this is due to his proximity to Caesar.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:45.190
Partly, it’s to do with Shakespeare.

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:49.470
Partly it’s probably also to do with Richard
Burton and Elizabeth Taylor.

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:52.750
But there’s more to this than simply luck.

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:55.970
Mark Antony’s story is a fascinating mix
of so many things.

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:57.340
It’s an adventure story.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:26:59.600
A story about how power corrupts.

00:26:59.600 --> 00:27:01.240
A love story for the ages.

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:05.690
A branching point for a million alternate
history tales.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:07.330
In short, it’s a myth.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:13.630
A myth where Godlike kings and emperors battle
and rule and fall in love, doing everything

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:17.429
on a scale far beyond normal human life.

00:27:17.429 --> 00:27:19.560
And yet, it really happened.

00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:25.120
The life of Mark Antony may have been a failure,
but it’s also one that speaks to us; as

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:29.400
poetic as any epic, as powerful as any legend.

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:34.820
Here, in the fires of love and war, the fate
of the known world was decided, changing our

00:27:34.820 --> 00:27:35.850
planet’s history.

00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:37.020
He may have never been emperor.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:39.500
He may have been outplayed by Octavian.

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.679
But Mark Antony was a historical colossus.

00:27:42.679 --> 00:27:49.000
A guy who did more and loved more in his lifetime
than most of us could ever dream of.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:28:04.000
In retelling his tale, we can experience just
a shadow of those great feelings too.

